4. CONTRIBUTIONS

"Contribution" means any work you deliberately submit for inclusion in the
Software -- a pull request, a patch, or anything else offered for the same
purpose. Reporting a bug, writing about the Software, or creating Content
under clause 3 is not a Contribution.

You keep the copyright in your Contribution. You are not assigning it.

By submitting a Contribution you grant the Licensor a perpetual, worldwide,
non-exclusive, royalty-free, irrevocable license to use, reproduce, modify,
distribute and sublicense it as part of the Software, under this license and
under any other terms the Licensor releases the Software under, including
commercial terms.

This grant runs to the Licensor only. It does not give any other person a
right to your Contribution beyond what clause 1 gives them in the Software as
a whole, and it does not permit the Licensor to relicense your Contribution
separately from the Software.

You confirm that you are legally able to make this grant -- that the work is
yours, or that whoever holds the rights has authorised you.

Why this clause exists, stated plainly: clause 5 lets the Licensor relicense
the Software, but that power reaches only the Licensor's own copyright.
Without an inbound grant, one accepted patch would leave a piece of the
Software that the Licensor cannot relicense and cannot remove without
rewriting it. This clause keeps that from happening. It asks for no more than
that.


